186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1065 3050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a0ce34c-f39a-5c24-bab3-840ff974402c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1064 "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1065 7628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d01e4be3-25c3-5922-9f88-c8eb7538e62e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119826 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form" #edb/ref 1