92186124480 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124480 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1045 "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124704 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "987e1e0c-bea1-5c9c-84ad-c50c09fe4abb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1061 17200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9d24104-f86a-5d95-8475-56e2dbec3b1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1061 17201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "791285ec-5594-542a-8408-63f1e171f030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125333 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1061 17202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a8ae7cc9-0379-5556-914f-78c73a269672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125334 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1061 17203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c86295bd-d79e-5f83-97d7-9a828e1b01e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125335 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1061 17204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bd0dda5-f697-5c86-9873-bdf79b5a2e7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125337 #edb/ref 1061 17205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125337 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "097f2290-0ddc-5eda-9104-3480eee4084e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125337 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1064 "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n![](images/ad/addf5cd1e1219a1e1ce28f3d27d1b000b3a99640ff89a62e55b66f3a5cdb3521.svg)\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/96/96dcf8cf9f0d387e0539ea04dd7ae4a498534361ad6bd5ed3bb119ae21f0416a.svg)\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1065 3157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "feec6418-f447-5ee0-8c62-7d6b690f9228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125757 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the pivots and pivot columns of a general matrix, we first need to convert the matrix to row echelon form (REF).\n\n- The pivots of any matrix are the pivots of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\n- The pivot columns of a matrix are the pivot columns of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\nTo reduce a general $n\\times m$ matrix to row echelon form, we follow a similar procedure to the $2\\times 2$ and $3\\times 3$ cases, as we'll see in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1065 3158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d45a4eba-96ce-5bef-92b7-b8f0d4cd8b72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125758 #edb/ref 1067 "Pivot Columns of a General Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Spheres, Hemispheres, Cones, and Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f4196423-3401-54e1-b214-9dbc4861cfe1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147611 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147611 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147611 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147611 #edb/ref 1039 "Naming Prisms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147611 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147611 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e8fd3c2c-3340-50df-a95c-3b82f165a071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147612 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147612 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147612 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147612 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147612 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Non-Polyhedra" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147612 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147612 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b9d83422-a877-5de8-bc55-2c7aae9eeb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147615 #edb/ref 1039 "Naming Pyramids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147615 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147615 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ddd0beef-ff0c-5ef3-88c4-ce49fbb8d5cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1045 "Identifying Three-Dimensional Shapes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186148590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147667 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "57371a77-6667-5bdd-b857-9b52cc9a92ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148323 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148323 #edb/ref 1061 33549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2317aeba-3862-5ebe-a106-96c1e216914d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148588 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148588 #edb/ref 1061 8908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148588 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19df8c7e-89cb-5578-919d-65b12d903d26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148588 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148589 #edb/ref 1061 8914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148589 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a01f473c-01ac-5a25-a94d-294c2cc662a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148589 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148590 #edb/ref 1061 8915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a54ee23c-aa2f-55da-8cdb-fd96e504ff67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148594 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148594 #edb/ref 1061 9198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148594 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e005ac2-7765-5ed0-83c6-dc038809de33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148594 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148595 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148595 #edb/ref 1061 9199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148595 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c62acce-9aa5-51e7-aab3-ddef11ded75b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148595 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148596 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148596 #edb/ref 1061 9201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148596 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9570e433-348e-5b8c-8ac5-b5e75693bf4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148596 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148714 #edb/ref 1064 "A **polyhedron** is a $3$-dimensional shape where each side is a polygon. These flat polygonal sides are called **faces**.\n\n**Prisms** are polyhedrons made of two identical polygonal faces (called **bases**) connected by equal parallel line segments. If these line segments are perpendicular to the bases, we have a **right** prism.\n\nLet's take a look at some common prisms.\n\n- A **triangular (right) prism** consists of two identical triangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\n![](images/95/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png)\n\n- A **rectangular (right) prism** consists of two identical rectangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments. Notice that each face is a rectangle.\n\n![](images/39/39ea3dd56722c7fb05ba275a054ff46479e3ee96851dafdd66fe09455a517f1a.png)\n\n- A **cube** is a special case of a rectangular prism that consists of six equal square faces.\n\n![](images/41/415d03a9a886793eee4b4d16e50af26002c0881279481b2e70773b8b7bff02a5.png)\n\nBy adding more sides to the polygonal base, we can make more prisms. For example, the following shape is a pentagonal prism.\n\n![](images/8f/8f4dd8fe8c120eabbe5a1ad6413f6f0cd9cd3d22efe8b13e25ac3d2a916f3258.png)\n\nWe can also construct hexagonal prisms, heptagonal prisms, etc." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148714 #edb/ref 1065 2202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148714 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "af52fff0-b45a-59d7-85f8-cacc3a9cb9f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148714 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148718 #edb/ref 1064 "**Pyramids** are polyhedrons made of a polygonal base, with the other triangular faces meeting at a single point.\n\nLet's take a look at some common pyramids.\n\n- A **triangular pyramid** has a triangular base with $3$ other triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/d8/d8597d1e526e2af72255f7dce1cc13e01cf76f1c53522003b126be67a0a6c55d.png)\n\n- A **rectangular pyramid** has a rectangular base with $4$ triangular faces that meet at a single point. If the base is a square, we call this a **square pyramid.**\n\n![](images/26/26700acaf9eae64227988ddcfb43269c0eba6f6447229ec8854cc1ebfeab3334.png)\n\n- A **pentagonal pyramid** has a pentagonal base with $5$ triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/4c/4c73b984b3e4a2ccb0796646ebb4c547947fab7359914599d928a63819818725.png)\n\nSimilarly to prisms, we can add more sides to the polygonal base to make more types of pyramids." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148718 #edb/ref 1065 2248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148718 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ebd5b00-1afa-5ee5-9321-47a3e76e71ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148718 #edb/ref 1067 "Pyramids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148719 #edb/ref 1064 "Not all three-dimensional shapes are polyhedrons. Let's see some examples.\n\n- A **sphere** is the set of points with the same distance to a fixed point called the **center.** A sphere is a $3$-dimensional analog of a circle. A **hemisphere** is half of a sphere.\n\n![](images/84/84e7b63b5ec8761d4a3ac8a00b15a08f0dab7d4f95a536721df0c82e2999873f.png)\n\n- A **cylinder** is a solid shape with two parallel circular bases. There are two types of cylinders.A cylinder is **right** if the axis that passes through the centers of the circular bases is perpendicular to the bases. Otherwise, the cylinder is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/f8/f8ddb161d2d8628dd1294cc0d0042437300787217b518be73f010e79be90dcf7.png)\n\n- A **cone** is a solid shape with one circular base and one vertex called the **apex**. There are two types of cones.A cone is **right** if the axis that passes through the center of the circular base and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. Otherwise, the cone is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/2f/2f9e9ca18a36336ed99695bb51b167ffbd83f002139728f8dfe15227bf9f7745.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148719 #edb/ref 1065 2250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148719 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "daf27141-b818-5e08-b09c-b940a0ab763a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148719 #edb/ref 1067 "Other Three-Dimensional Shapes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260725 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-4},\\,z=-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260725 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260725 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "628ba019-e287-5e6c-9c20-0bd08a8d96f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261472 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261472 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261472 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b5e6a649-3a81-512d-94bb-f14eb562c95d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261635 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261635 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261635 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7117f4f-8b9b-5207-8fcb-870aceb867f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261871 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e335a903-50df-50af-bbcb-1934b2874ec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262020 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{-4},\\,z=-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262020 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262020 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f563cde3-4746-533c-bf25-d120ecc2da8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186262020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186261472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262169 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1c9a7cd2-cbdc-54f5-8320-283acf5adf7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449179 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449180 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449181 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "364a18ef-2de8-57d7-9e2f-631eb4651446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262854 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263117 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263117 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263117 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c423f32-6ae0-5d69-9e82-133ca26c00b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263181 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263181 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263181 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13016ec0-8156-589e-b39b-942c97ca1edf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921862